From: steelhead99x <steelhead99x@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:31:51 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] perf(portable): rolled SHA-512 on Arm without CAS
 (ARMv6-M)

HACL*'s fully unrolled SHA-512 compiles to about 27 KB of Thumb code
for thumbv6m-none-eabi. That is larger than an RP2040's 16 KB XIP cache,
so each block refetched most of the function from QSPI flash: on a
Raspberry Pi Pico every library (rind, RustCrypto, libcrux) ran SHA-512
at about 2,200 cycles per byte, against 101 for SHA-256.

On Arm targets without CAS atomics (ARMv6-M, plus the tier-3 ARMv4T and
ARMv5TE bare-metal targets; target_has_atomic = "ptr" is the only cfg
stable Rust gives that tells them apart) the portable backend now takes
generic::sha512, a rolled loop of rounds in the style of
generic::sha256. Its compression function is 1,724 bytes there. Every
index and branch depends only on the round number. Every other target
keeps HACL*'s SHA-512.

On the Pico (125 MHz, XIP cache on, interleaved rounds, median of
per-round medians):

  sha512/rind/16KB           36.06 M -> 3.42 M cycles (0.095x)
  sha512/rind/32B            315.0 k -> 28.7 k cycles (0.091x)
  hmac-sha384/rind/32B       1.195 M -> 114.6 k cycles (0.096x)
  hkdf-sha384/expand/rind    625.3 k -> 57.8 k cycles (0.092x)
  ed25519-sign/rind          7.87 M -> 7.28 M cycles (0.925x)

A test checks it against the backend's own SHA-512 over 1 to 9 blocks.

Co-authored-by: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
